.TH @NAME@ 1 "2026-08-03" "@NAME@ 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
@NAME@ \- drag-and-drop source/target with a history of past runs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B @NAME@
[\fIOPTION\fR...] [\fIFILE\fR|\fIURI\fR...]
.br
.B @NAME@
.SH DESCRIPTION
Many applications, particularly web applications and chat clients, expect files
to be dragged into them. If you do not habitually use Finder that is a problem.
.B @NAME@
opens a small window holding the files you name, ready to be dragged wherever
you need them:
.PP
.RS
@NAME@ file.tar.gz
.RE
.PP
A dragged file is offered as a file URL and nothing else, the same as Finder, so
anything that accepts a file from Finder accepts it from @NAME@ - including
sandboxed Mac Catalyst applications such as WhatsApp, which refuse a file drag
that also advertises plain text. A dragged URI is offered as a URL and as plain
text.
.PP
To drag files \fIin\fR instead, use
.B \-\-target\fR:
dropped items are printed to standard output.
.PP
Run with no arguments,
.B @NAME@
shows the history of previous runs. Each entry expands into the items it held,
and any of them can be dragged straight out of the list or reopened as a drag
source. History is kept in
.IR ~/Library/Application\ Support/Drake/history.json .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-and\-exit ", " \-x
Exit after a single completed drop.
.TP
.BR \-\-target ", " \-t
Act as a target instead of a source.
.TP
.BR \-\-keep ", " \-k
With \fB\-\-target\fR, keep dropped files so they can be dragged out again.
.TP
.BR \-\-print\-path ", " \-p
With \fB\-\-target\fR, print file paths instead of URIs.
.TP
.BR \-\-all ", " \-a
Drag all files at once.
.TP
.BR \-\-all\-compact ", " \-A
Drag all files at once, only displaying the number of files.
.TP
.BR \-\-icon\-only ", " \-i
Only show icons.
.TP
.BR \-\-name\-only ", " \-f
Only show each file's basename, not its path.
.TP
.BR \-\-full\-path ", " \-F
Show the absolute path to all files.
.TP
.BR \-\-on\-top ", " \-T
Keep the window above other windows.
The window always appears on every Space (desktop), with or without this option.
.TP
.BR \-\-stdin ", " \-I
Read filenames from standard input.
.TP
.BR \-\-thumb\-size ", " \-s " " \fISIZE\fR
Set thumbnail size, 16 to 512 (default 96).
.TP
.BR \-\-verbose ", " \-v
Be verbose on standard error.
.TP
.BR \-\-history ", " \-H
Browse the history of previous runs even when files are given.
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-history ", " \-N
Do not record this run in the history.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-animation
Open the window without the system's window-opening animation.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show help.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show version details.
.SH SELECTION
Everything starts selected, so one drag sends the whole list. Click a row to
select just that row, Command-click to toggle rows, Shift-click to extend a
range, and press and sweep in empty space to rubber-band select. Dragging any
selected row carries the whole selection.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B Cmd-A
Select all.
.TP
.B Cmd-O
Open the selection.
.TP
.B Cmd-R
Reveal the selection in Finder.
.TP
.B Cmd-C
Copy the selection's paths.
.TP
.B Cmd-Y
Show the history.
.TP
.B Cmd-[
Go back from the history to the files.
.TP
.B Cmd-F
Search the history, from any screen.
.TP
.B Delete
Remove the selected rows from the list.
.TP
.B Up/Down
Move the selection; hold Shift to extend it.
.TP
.BR Escape ", " q ", " Cmd-W
Close. Escape and \fBq\fR work anywhere except while typing in a text field
(the history search, a rename), where Escape leaves the field first and
\fBq\fR types a q.
.B q
also works in the terminal drake was started from, while it is the
foreground job.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Drag a single file and quit once it lands:
.B @NAME@ \-x report.pdf
.TP
Collect files dropped from elsewhere:
.B @NAME@ \-\-target \-\-keep
.TP
Send everything in a directory as one drag:
.B @NAME@ \-\-all *.png
.TP
Take filenames from a pipe:
.B find . \-name '*.log' | @NAME@ \-\-stdin
.TP
Reopen something dragged earlier:
.B @NAME@
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/Library/Application Support/Drake/history.json
The history of previous runs.
.TP
.I ~/Library/Logs/Drake/drake.log
Warnings and errors, rotated at 256 KB. Help > Show Problem Log opens it.
Everything else goes to the unified log under the subsystem
.BR com.marcos.Drake :
.B log show \-\-last 1h \-\-predicate 'subsystem == "com.marcos.Drake"'
.SH EXIT STATUS
Exits 0 normally, 1 on a bad option or a file that does not exist.
.SH SEE ALSO
dragon(1), the Linux program this is modelled on:
.I https://github.com/mwh/dragon
.SH AUTHOR
Written for macOS in Swift, after mwh's dragon for X and Wayland.
